{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":19922857,"ns":0,"title":"Scalar implicature","extract":"In pragmatics, scalar implicature, or quantity implicature, is an implicature that attributes an implicit meaning beyond the explicit or literal meaning of an utterance, and which suggests that the utterer had a reason for not using a more informative or stronger term on the same scale.  The choice of the weaker characterization suggests that, as far as the speaker knows, none of the stronger characterizations in the scale holds.","description":"Term in linguistics","descriptionsource":"local"}]}}